 ## Overview
-Inference Ray Marching and Integration is a GPU-accelerated pipeline for real-time volume rendering inference, designed to generate images from trained neural radiance fields (NeRFs) or sparse density grids without gradient computation [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently traversing sparse volumetric data to sample points and integrate radiance, utilizing density bitfields or occupancy grids for acceleration and early termination to maximize throughput [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It differs from training-time marchers by disabling gradient flow, using fixed or pre-computed step sizes, and operating on pre-computed ray samples with strict early-termination logic to prioritize latency over optimization stability [S2, S3, unofficial]. It serves as the core rendering engine, bridging sparse ray traversal and final compositing [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
